\section*{Motivation}

A well-designed perennial planting may appear natural and effortless, but it is the result of many deliberate decisions: which species grow next to one another, how many individuals are planted together, and how the planting changes through the flowering season. These decisions shape not only the look of a planting but also its ecological value, because the choice of plants determines which flower-visiting insects find food. In the example data, several target bees are specialized on a few plant taxa, for instance \textit{Macropis europaea} and \textit{Andrena viridescens}, which the upstream report classifies as strictly oligolectic, so suitable flowering plants matter especially in urban green spaces. In the planning pipeline provided for this project, an upstream optimizer already selects perennials and their quantities for a given site and a set of target bee taxa. The example list contains 17 taxa that together support 16 of the 18 target bee taxa.

A list of taxa and quantities, however, is not yet a planting plan. The same list can become a coherent and useful planting, or an arrangement in which tall species dominate smaller plants, structural plants gather in one corner and flowering is unevenly distributed. Designers work with functional roles such as structural plants, companions and ground cover, and with sociability levels that tell whether a species belongs in groups or stands alone; both are part of the trait data used here. These principles are mostly qualitative, they can conflict with one another, and they must be applied to areas with paths, holes and irregular outlines. Space is also a real constraint: with the recommended planting distances, the 100 plants of the example list need roughly 17\,m$^2$ of a 50\,m$^2$ bed, while the target of 500 plants would need an estimated 82\,m$^2$, far more than the bed offers. The target therefore stays at 500 plants, but the area is to be filled with as many plants as possible while keeping the relative frequencies of the taxa as constant as possible.

This project translates selected design principles into formal rules and uses them to arrange a fixed plant list on a gridded area. The area is a 20\,cm grid with masks for paths and holes, hard constraints keep every plan valid, and weighted soft rules express design preferences. A two-stage algorithm then builds a plan by greedy insertion and improves it by Simulated Annealing.\cite{kirkpatrick} Turning design guidelines into an energy function and minimizing it has worked for furniture layouts,\cite{merrell2011,yu2011} but perennial planting adds its own requirements: the species are predetermined, plant traits shape spatial relationships, and the result must remain horticulturally plausible.

The expected outcome is a reproducible tool that converts a plant list and a masked area into a feasible coordinate plan with as many plants as the area can hold, together with a documented catalogue of the design rules that could be formalized with the available traits. The evaluation shows how much Simulated Annealing improves on greedy insertion alone and whether this holds across area geometries.

\section*{Task Description}

The task is to place plants, not to select them. The taxa come from the upstream optimizer and stay fixed. The target is 500 plants per area. Since this many plants do not fit at the recommended spacing, the area is filled with as many plants as possible while the relative frequencies of the taxa are kept as constant as possible. Areas are numerical arrays with a cell size of 20\,cm $\times$ 20\,cm, and masks turn them into arbitrary geometries. Three test areas are used: a plain rectangle as reference, an area crossed by a path, and an area with a large hole. The program takes plant lists and area masks as separate inputs, so that every supplied list can be run on every area. Development starts with the example list C.

\paragraph{Research question.}
How can design rules for mixed perennial plantings be formalized so that greedy insertion followed by Simulated Annealing produces feasible plans that satisfy hard spatial constraints and score better on defined design rules than greedy insertion alone?

\begin{enumerate}[label=RQ\arabic*,leftmargin=*,itemsep=0.2em]
  \item Which design rules can be formalized with the available trait data, and should each be a hard constraint or a weighted rule?
  \item How many plants can an area hold at the chosen planting distances, and how can it be filled with as many plants as possible, up to the target of 500, while keeping the relative frequencies of the taxa as constant as possible?
  \item How much does Simulated Annealing improve on greedy insertion, and how consistent is this across random seeds and area geometries?
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Hypotheses.}
The hypotheses follow from the two-stage design and can be rejected by the experiments.

\begin{enumerate}[label=H\arabic*,leftmargin=*,itemsep=0.2em]
  \item Simulated Annealing reaches a lower best-so-far energy than its own greedy starting plan, with a positive median relative improvement in every area. The gain is expected to be larger on the areas with path and hole, where narrow regions make early placements more restrictive.
  \item The gain is consistent: in at least 75\,\% of the runs of an area, the final energy is lower than that of the greedy start.
  \item Inserting plants in order of constraint (fewest feasible cells and largest spacing first) gives a lower greedy energy and fewer failed placements than a random order.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Related work.}
Planting design knowledge is documented mostly in qualitative form in the horticultural literature, which the literature review in the first project phase will survey for rules that can be formalized. In interior design, layout methods encode design guidelines as energy terms and optimize them, for example for furniture arrangements.\cite{merrell2011,yu2011} The algorithmic basis of this project is Simulated Annealing.\cite{kirkpatrick} The literature review will be extended with journal articles on plant neighborhood effects, planting density, pollinator resources and existing computational planting methods, and every source cited in the final report will have been read.

