\section*{Scientific Approach}

\paragraph{Sources and data.}
The starting material is example list C, a long-format trait table for 17 taxa (height, flowering period, planting distance, sociability level, growth habit, habitat), together with the report and configuration of the upstream optimizer, which give the quantities, the target and first parameter values. Candidate design rules are collected in the literature review. The data have gaps: planting distance is missing for \textit{Cichorium intybus} var.\ \textit{sativum} (27 plants) and \textit{Epilobium angustifolium} (3 plants), together 30 of the 100 plants, leaf height is missing for most taxa, and the report lists no bulbs although the configuration asks for at least three. A soft rule simply skips taxa without its required trait. Spacing, however, is a hard constraint and needs a value for every plant, so a documented fallback from the closest related taxon is used, its share of plants is reported, and it is part of the sensitivity analysis.

\paragraph{Quantities and filling (RQ2).}
The target is $N_{\mathrm{target}}=500$ plants per area, and the area is to be filled with as many plants as possible while the relative frequencies of the taxa stay as close as possible to those of the supplied list. For any total $N$, the largest-remainder method gives the integer counts whose proportions deviate least from the supplied ones. A quick screen compares the plantable area with the space the plants need if each occupies a disc of radius $d_i/2$,
\begin{equation}
  A_{\mathrm{discs}} = \sum_i \pi \left(\frac{d_i}{2}\right)^2 .
\end{equation}
This is only a necessary indication, because irregular geometries and mixed sizes can make a placement impossible even when the discs would fit. The definitive test is the placement itself. A capacity search takes the largest $N\le 500$ for which greedy insertion places all plants in all seeds, found by bisection followed by single steps. This $N$ is then kept fixed, so that greedy insertion and Simulated Annealing place identical plants. For list C the screen gives about 17\,m$^2$ for the 100 listed plants and about 82\,m$^2$ for 500 plants at the minimum spacing, so the capacity search is expected to return a smaller $N$. The 16 \textit{Lathyrus pratensis} plants with a spacing of 80\,cm or more contribute most. Whether the recommended distances are strict minima in mixed plantings, which are usually planted denser, remains a question for the supervisor.

\paragraph{Rule model (RQ1).}
Hard constraints make a plan valid. A plant centre lies on a plantable cell and at least $d_i/2$ from the boundary of the plantable region, including the edges of paths and holes, computed with a Euclidean distance transform on the mask. Two plants $i$ and $j$ must satisfy
\begin{equation}
  D_{ij} \geq \frac{d_i+d_j}{2},
\end{equation}
with $D_{ij}$ the Euclidean distance between their centres and $d_i$ the lower Pflanzabstand value. This is a modeling assumption, not a biological law, and the stricter $\max(d_i,d_j)$ is tested as an alternative. The soft rules are combined in the energy
\begin{equation}
  E(X) = \sum_k w_k E_k(X),
\end{equation}
where $X$ contains the plant positions, $w_k$ is the weight of rule $k$, and each penalty $E_k\in[0,1]$ has zero as best value. Only rules with a horticultural rationale and sufficient trait data are included, as listed in Table~\ref{tab:rules}.

