Flowering across the whole season is fixed by the taxa and is only reported, while the spatial rule checks that flowers are available everywhere. Colour distribution is optional and is added only if literature and data support a reproducible definition. Rules can conflict, for example compact groups against an even spread, so every term is reported separately. The weights are modeling parameters: the baseline uses equal weights, and the sensitivity experiments show how plans and the algorithm comparison change with them.

\paragraph{Optimization algorithm.}
Greedy insertion processes the plants in order of constraint: fewest feasible cells, largest spacing, most restrictive grouping, with the functional category only as a secondary criterion. Ties are broken at random, so the starting plan depends on the seed. Each plant goes to the admissible cell with the lowest resulting energy, and a run is marked infeasible if no admissible cell remains. Simulated Annealing then starts from this plan and proposes to shift a plant to a nearby admissible cell, to swap two plants of different taxa, or to move a group of plants together. A swap must satisfy the spacing at both new positions, and every proposal that violates a hard constraint is rejected. A feasible proposal with energy change $\Delta E$ is accepted with probability
\begin{equation}
  P(\mathrm{accept}) = \min\left(1,\exp\left(-\frac{\Delta E}{T}\right)\right),
\end{equation}
and the temperature $T$ decreases geometrically.\cite{kirkpatrick} The values of the upstream configuration ($T_0=10$, cooling rate $0.9995$, 20\,000 iterations) are starting points for tuning on pilot runs. Since a move changes only a local neighborhood, $\Delta E$ is computed incrementally with a spatial lookup, and the best plan found is always retained.

\paragraph{Experiments and evaluation (RQ3).}
The implementation uses Python with NumPy and SciPy, is configuration-driven and records all random seeds. Each configuration runs with 20 seeds on every area, and on every supplied plant list. Table~\ref{tab:experiments} lists the experiments. As a budget check, greedy insertion with random restarts and the same number of energy evaluations as one annealing run is also compared with Simulated Annealing.

